The ultimate St. Patrick's Day feast made foolproof with a pressure cooker. Instead of simmering for four long hours on the stovetop, the Instant Pot renders tough corned beef brisket fork-tender in just 90 minutes. A clever two-stage cooking method ensures the cabbage wedges, baby potatoes, and carrots cook in the seasoned beef broth during a lightning-fast second cycle so they emerge perfectly tender without turning soggy.

*Adapted from [Delish](https://www.delish.com/cooking/recipe-ideas/a26243431/instant-pot-corned-beef-recipe/).*

## Ingredients

### Braised Corned Beef
- 1 large yellow onion, peeled and cut into 8 thick wedges
- 1 flat-cut corned beef brisket (approx. 2 to 3 lbs), with enclosed pickling spice packet
- 4 cups low-sodium chicken broth (or beef broth or water)

### Braised Vegetables
- 1 small head green cabbage, outer leaves discarded, cored, and sliced into 6 to 8 wedges
- 1 lb baby red or Yukon gold potatoes, scrubbed and halved
- 4 medium carrots, peeled and cut on the diagonal into 2-inch chunks
- 3 tablespoons extra-virgin olive oil
- Freshly cracked black pepper, to taste
- 2 tablespoons fresh flat-leaf parsley, finely chopped (for garnish)
- Coarse stone-ground or Dijon mustard (for serving)

## Instructions

1. **Layer the Meat and Broth:** Place the thick onion wedges into the base of a 6-quart Instant Pot to form an aromatic bed. Lay the corned beef brisket directly on top of the onions with the fat cap facing up. Empty the enclosed pickling spice packet (containing mustard seeds, coriander, peppercorns, and bay leaf) over the surface of the meat. Pour the 4 cups of broth around the brisket.
2. **Pressure Cook the Corned Beef:** Lock the Instant Pot lid in place and turn the steam release valve to **Sealing**. Cook on **HIGH pressure for 90 minutes**.
3. **Quick Release and Rest Beef:** As soon as the 90-minute timer beeps, turn the steam release valve to **Venting** to perform a quick pressure release. Once the floating float valve drops, unlock the lid. Use tongs to lift the tender corned beef brisket and cooked onions onto a carving board or serving platter. Tent tightly with aluminum foil to keep warm and rest while the vegetables cook. Do not discard the flavorful cooking liquid left in the pot!
4. **Cook the Vegetables (Second Stage):** Add the halved baby potatoes, sliced carrots, and cabbage wedges into the hot cooking liquid remaining in the pot. Drizzle the olive oil over the vegetables and sprinkle with freshly cracked black pepper (skip adding salt, as the corned beef liquid is already seasoned).
5. **Quick Vegetable Pressure Cycle:** Lock the lid back on the Instant Pot, turn the valve to **Sealing**, and pressure cook on **HIGH for 4 minutes**.
6. **Immediate Quick Release:** Immediately flip the valve to **Venting** when the 4-minute timer beeps. Discharging the steam right away stops the cooking process instantly, ensuring the cabbage retains its shape and bright sweetness while the potatoes and carrots are fork-tender.
7. **Carve Against the Grain:** Uncover the corned beef. Examine the surface to identify the direction of the muscle grain. Use a sharp slicing knife to carve the brisket into thin slices across (perpendicular to) the grain.
8. **Platter and Serve:** Arrange the sliced beef down the center of a large heated platter, surrounded by the tender cabbage wedges, carrots, and potatoes. Ladle several generous spoonfuls of the warm cooking broth over the beef to keep it glistening and moist. Scatter chopped fresh parsley over the platter and serve hot alongside stone-ground mustard.

## Notes

- **Why Two Stages?** Corned beef brisket requires high heat and pressure for 90 minutes to break down dense collagen, while tender vegetables like cabbage and carrots become waterlogged mush if cooked longer than 4 to 5 minutes. Cooking in two quick stages guarantees restaurant-quality results for both.
- **Leftover Corned Beef:** Any extra brisket makes exceptional homemade Reuben sandwiches with Swiss cheese, sauerkraut, and Russian dressing on toasted rye.
